1
00:00:59,296 --> 00:00:59,961
[SPEAKER_06]: I love how Max starts out his day.

2
00:02:22,114 --> 00:02:22,658
[SPEAKER_05]: I'll show you.

3
00:03:12,415 --> 00:03:13,777
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, check, check, there we go.

4
00:03:14,558 --> 00:03:15,739
[SPEAKER_03]: Good afternoon everybody.

5
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:17,522
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you so much for coming out.

6
00:03:17,602 --> 00:03:19,925
[SPEAKER_03]: The seventh annual Medford Jazz Festival.

7
00:03:20,245 --> 00:03:22,448
[SPEAKER_03]: The fourth year it's been on the back patio.

8
00:03:22,508 --> 00:03:24,270
[SPEAKER_03]: It just keeps getting better every single time.

9
00:03:25,331 --> 00:03:29,276
[SPEAKER_03]: So it's great to see some folks that have been here in past years and some that haven't.

10
00:03:30,177 --> 00:03:32,058
[SPEAKER_03]: Welcome to the West Medford Community Center.

11
00:03:32,099 --> 00:03:37,602
[SPEAKER_03]: I know Terry Carter is going to tell you more about that, but in the meantime, my name is Jonathan Fagan.

12
00:03:37,902 --> 00:03:49,350
[SPEAKER_03]: I've been leading this for, well, ever since 2020, which obviously was a very different year for live music, but this has been such a joy to bring all of these local artists here.

13
00:03:50,270 --> 00:03:58,134
[SPEAKER_03]: Before we get started, I'll leave it to Terry to introduce the group, but just need to thank our sponsors, because none of this would be possible without them.

14
00:03:58,694 --> 00:04:05,197
[SPEAKER_03]: So thank you to Medford Arts Council, to the, yes, we can clap for every single one of them if we want to.

15
00:04:06,138 --> 00:04:13,201
[SPEAKER_03]: The Arts Alive Foundation, yeah, headed by the great May Markabrook, who is just an amazing cultural ambassador for the city.

16
00:04:14,261 --> 00:04:17,363
[SPEAKER_03]: For Berkeley's Institute of Global Jazz, you'll hear from them tomorrow.

17
00:04:19,020 --> 00:04:35,657
[SPEAKER_03]: uh... and also berkeley's institute for jazz and gender justice late led by a great terry lynn kerrington who grew up right around here to have been i was enjoy telling people about that we don't know to local music schools also in winchester community music school

18
00:04:36,523 --> 00:04:39,805
[SPEAKER_03]: Right down the street, pretty much, Morningside Music Studio.

19
00:04:40,165 --> 00:04:42,887
[SPEAKER_03]: Heard a couple of their students last night with the Vertigo Trio.

20
00:04:44,167 --> 00:04:46,549
[SPEAKER_03]: And also to all of our Patreon subscribers.

21
00:04:46,989 --> 00:04:49,150
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, we all have too many monthly subscriptions.

22
00:04:49,190 --> 00:04:50,491
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, Patreon is a good one.

23
00:04:50,871 --> 00:04:51,352
[SPEAKER_03]: I promise.

24
00:04:52,792 --> 00:04:54,192
[SPEAKER_03]: That's just a few dollars a month.

25
00:04:54,252 --> 00:05:01,454
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a normal subscription service, but it helps us sustain jam sessions and also fund all of the artists that are performing at our festival.

26
00:05:02,234 --> 00:05:10,335
[SPEAKER_03]: To a couple of other individual donors, including Susan Klein, Steve Shulman, and Bob Bean, they've really pushed us over the edge.

27
00:05:10,735 --> 00:05:11,015
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

28
00:05:11,775 --> 00:05:21,577
[SPEAKER_03]: And then I'm trying to make sure that I don't, oh yeah, and of course the services of Audience Sound Company, which Avi Fagan over there.

29
00:05:22,637 --> 00:05:28,460
[SPEAKER_03]: To Medford Community Media with Jim over there manning some, and Bruce also over there on the camera.

30
00:05:28,480 --> 00:05:29,640
[SPEAKER_03]: Let me not forget about him.

31
00:05:30,701 --> 00:05:31,801
[SPEAKER_03]: He's also on our board.

32
00:05:33,362 --> 00:05:38,644
[SPEAKER_03]: And lastly, and most importantly, the West Medford Community Center for supporting us here.

33
00:05:40,505 --> 00:05:43,466
[SPEAKER_03]: We have a food vendor inside in the Danish Pastry House.

34
00:05:44,267 --> 00:05:46,708
[SPEAKER_03]: So if you haven't gotten anything already, they're really good.

35
00:05:48,049 --> 00:05:52,071
[SPEAKER_03]: So maybe during a break between the sets, I'm sure you won't want to miss any of this.

36
00:05:53,331 --> 00:06:00,173
[SPEAKER_03]: And last to a board of directors, that's been a real joy just to get some help in all the facets of planning this.

37
00:06:00,793 --> 00:06:04,875
[SPEAKER_03]: Shayla McDermott, who's right there in action passing out programs to people.

38
00:06:06,553 --> 00:06:33,210
[SPEAKER_03]: uh... to bruce and i mentioned over there in the cameras recently live or up men in the t-shirt station uh... and to our yet to our two volunteers i love how intergenerational this is uh... we've got to kota over there helping out um... go well who's over there helping out with sound and yet because uh... and roman see there are too many i can even keep track of them all yeah

39
00:06:34,611 --> 00:06:37,394
[SPEAKER_03]: So with that, I'm going to turn it over to Terry Carter.

40
00:06:37,414 --> 00:06:41,738
[SPEAKER_03]: He's going to tell you a little bit about the community center and introduce this first group.

41
00:06:46,113 --> 00:06:56,502
[SPEAKER_01]: So I like to say that Jonathan and I kind of met at what we refer to as the intersection of jazz and social justice.

42
00:06:56,623 --> 00:07:04,650
[SPEAKER_01]: And you'll hear a little bit more about that tomorrow when we play the last set of the third day of the Jazz Festival.

43
00:07:05,030 --> 00:07:06,932
[SPEAKER_01]: We had a wonderful evening.

44
00:07:07,933 --> 00:07:08,433
[SPEAKER_01]: It was just...

45
00:07:09,454 --> 00:07:12,296
[SPEAKER_01]: spectacular music, a great crowd.

46
00:07:12,737 --> 00:07:17,300
[SPEAKER_01]: So we're really, really excited about what we have to present to you today.

47
00:07:17,680 --> 00:07:20,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Just a quick word about the West Medford Community Center.

48
00:07:20,883 --> 00:07:22,904
[SPEAKER_01]: We've been here for over 90 years.

49
00:07:22,984 --> 00:07:25,286
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the second building on this site.

50
00:07:25,967 --> 00:07:36,395
[SPEAKER_01]: And the West Medford community has been the traditional heart of the African-American presence in West Medford, in Medford proper.

51
00:07:37,155 --> 00:07:44,383
[SPEAKER_01]: And Duggar Park was named after Lieutenant Colonel Edward Duggar, who was one of our local heroes.

52
00:07:45,424 --> 00:07:52,992
[SPEAKER_01]: But this building, 111 Arlington Street, really, really represents the home for

53
00:07:53,973 --> 00:07:58,894
[SPEAKER_01]: West Medford's black community when it really, really was without a home.

54
00:07:59,395 --> 00:08:04,756
[SPEAKER_01]: When the community first started, there were really only three streets that people of color could live on.

55
00:08:04,816 --> 00:08:11,418
[SPEAKER_01]: It was Arlington Street, which is right behind us, there was Lincoln Street, the street over from that, and then there was Jerome Street.

56
00:08:11,918 --> 00:08:15,339
[SPEAKER_01]: We were redlined out of everywhere else in Medford.

57
00:08:15,419 --> 00:08:18,220
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's where this community began.

58
00:08:18,240 --> 00:08:29,403
[SPEAKER_01]: And in the 30s, the men got together and decided that they wanted to have something they could call their own, where they could gather and socialize and so forth.

59
00:08:30,464 --> 00:08:36,906
[SPEAKER_01]: But by 1945, they realized that this whole notion of men alone wasn't going to work.

60
00:08:38,246 --> 00:08:40,447
[SPEAKER_01]: And the womanly influence took over.

61
00:08:40,567 --> 00:08:44,529
[SPEAKER_01]: So around 1945, those men got together.

62
00:08:45,049 --> 00:08:51,892
[SPEAKER_01]: They went and got a building, a Quonset hut or old military barracks from Boston.

63
00:08:52,272 --> 00:08:56,134
[SPEAKER_01]: They carted it here on one of the men's moving trucks.

64
00:08:56,734 --> 00:09:01,617
[SPEAKER_01]: And they poured a foundation and built the old community center on this site.

65
00:09:01,977 --> 00:09:04,538
[SPEAKER_01]: And as I say, this is the second building on the site.

66
00:09:05,018 --> 00:09:11,521
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, it represents over 90 years of African-American history in the city of Medford.

67
00:09:11,541 --> 00:09:22,346
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you go inside, and hopefully you will, if nothing else but to gather some food from Danish Pastry Hut, you'll notice two levels of history.

68
00:09:22,946 --> 00:09:25,267
[SPEAKER_01]: The top level is the West Medford Elder Project.

69
00:09:25,727 --> 00:09:29,988
[SPEAKER_01]: The level below that is the West Medford Afro-American Remembrance Project.

70
00:09:30,348 --> 00:09:34,529
[SPEAKER_01]: And it tells really, really the lion's share of the history of this community.

71
00:09:34,589 --> 00:09:38,470
[SPEAKER_01]: So hopefully, you'll go inside and avail yourself of some of that history.

72
00:09:38,990 --> 00:09:41,731
[SPEAKER_01]: All right, now to the matter at hand.

73
00:09:42,051 --> 00:09:46,272
[SPEAKER_01]: So I have known Farahi Malik for

74
00:09:48,298 --> 00:09:50,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it seven, eight years now?

75
00:09:51,061 --> 00:09:51,862
[SPEAKER_01]: It's probably that.

76
00:09:52,202 --> 00:10:06,497
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and our story began as Farai being one of the singers at one of our mutual friends Stacey Clayton's annual gospel concerts over at Somerville Community Baptist Church.

77
00:10:07,557 --> 00:10:17,983
[SPEAKER_01]: And Stacey had a way of, and I don't know how she did it, but she had a way of getting aspiring superstars to come sing in her choir, right?

78
00:10:18,103 --> 00:10:20,665
[SPEAKER_01]: There have been several of them, and Farai is one of them.

79
00:10:20,725 --> 00:10:25,728
[SPEAKER_01]: And not only aspiring superstar singers, but also musicians.

80
00:10:25,808 --> 00:10:30,831
[SPEAKER_01]: And some of the musicians back here are musicians that came out of that tradition as well.

81
00:10:31,391 --> 00:10:34,533
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's Farai Malik on vocals and acoustic guitar.

82
00:10:36,070 --> 00:10:38,891
[SPEAKER_01]: It's Grayson Hillen on vocals and electric bass.

83
00:10:39,711 --> 00:10:42,912
[SPEAKER_01]: It's Jacob Means on vocals and the mandolin.

84
00:10:43,012 --> 00:10:44,572
[SPEAKER_01]: So you're in for a treat there.

85
00:10:45,673 --> 00:10:50,134
[SPEAKER_01]: My man, Max Ridley on the big sexy, the big upright bass.

86
00:10:51,234 --> 00:10:54,955
[SPEAKER_01]: And Hector Falu Guzman on the drum set, okay.

87
00:10:55,611 --> 00:10:55,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Grayson?

88
00:10:56,452 --> 00:10:56,712
[SPEAKER_01]: OK.

89
00:10:56,792 --> 00:10:57,052
[SPEAKER_01]: All right.

90
00:10:57,072 --> 00:10:58,533
[SPEAKER_01]: So I think I got everybody.

91
00:10:59,014 --> 00:11:04,498
[SPEAKER_01]: So now what I do is I get out of the way, and I let the professional musicians take over.

92
00:11:04,798 --> 00:11:07,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Ladies and gentlemen, the Farahi Malik Quintet.

93
00:11:13,566 --> 00:11:14,606
[SPEAKER_02]: How's everybody doing?

94
00:11:15,527 --> 00:11:16,188
[SPEAKER_02]: We doing all right?

95
00:11:25,398 --> 00:11:27,299
[SPEAKER_02]: Thank you so much, Terry, for having us.

96
00:11:27,359 --> 00:11:28,540
[SPEAKER_02]: We're so glad to be here.

97
00:11:28,760 --> 00:11:32,442
[SPEAKER_02]: And we're excited to be playing this set of music for you today.

98
00:11:32,483 --> 00:11:46,812
[SPEAKER_02]: So we're going to do some music, a couple tunes off my first EP, Edge of the Earth, that came out last year, and then some new singles that I have out, and some music that we're about to record tomorrow in Nashville with this guy.

99
00:11:47,252 --> 00:11:47,892
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh my goodness.

100
00:11:48,032 --> 00:11:49,954
[SPEAKER_02]: So we both have very early morning flights.

101
00:11:51,374 --> 00:11:55,216
[SPEAKER_02]: But we're really excited to play this new music for you, because not very many audiences have heard it.

102
00:11:55,316 --> 00:11:58,398
[SPEAKER_02]: So if you like the new music, tell us yes.

103
00:11:58,578 --> 00:12:00,479
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you don't, don't tell us.

104
00:12:02,900 --> 00:12:05,222
[SPEAKER_02]: This first one is called Every Heartbeat.

105
00:12:05,242 --> 00:12:08,023
[SPEAKER_02]: It's my latest, or second to last single.

106
00:12:08,063 --> 00:12:09,244
[SPEAKER_02]: This came out last November.

107
00:12:22,322 --> 00:12:40,115
[SPEAKER_08]: I came through a valley cold one winter night and shining on a hill a yellow beam of light gazing in my wonder

108
00:12:41,390 --> 00:13:10,427
[SPEAKER_08]: Through the frosted trees It felt so real I questioned if it was a dream Walking under moonlight

109
00:13:15,538 --> 00:13:36,791
[SPEAKER_08]: Feelings absent from my heart for years Now every color is brighter, all I smell is sweet I feel the gentle snow fall softly on my cheek Something I was missing finally complete I'm seeing with brand new eyes and I feel you in every heartbeat

110
00:13:52,356 --> 00:14:04,668
[SPEAKER_08]: Standing now beside you, falling into your embrace, I'm understood and love completely for all day.

111
00:14:07,031 --> 00:14:13,277
[SPEAKER_08]: From my shining hour to my darkest night,

112
00:14:26,423 --> 00:14:31,519
[SPEAKER_08]: But something I was missing, finally a complete break I'm singing with brand new eyes and

113
00:14:55,858 --> 00:15:19,858
[SPEAKER_08]: I feel you in every heartbeat I came through a valley cold on winter night and shining on a hill a yellow beam of light

114
00:15:27,990 --> 00:15:28,732
[SPEAKER_02]: Thank you so much.

115
00:15:31,197 --> 00:15:35,727
[SPEAKER_02]: This next one is called In Her Closet, and it's off my EP, Edge of the Earth.

116
00:15:36,088 --> 00:15:37,170
[SPEAKER_02]: And this is about how

117
00:15:40,334 --> 00:15:43,376
[SPEAKER_02]: We all have these little parts of ourselves that we don't necessarily want the world to see.

118
00:15:43,396 --> 00:15:57,086
[SPEAKER_02]: It might be a problem that we're dealing with, a struggle, maybe something in our past, something in our present, but as we grow into ourselves and we grow to love who we are, it becomes harder and harder to hide those parts because we just want to be seen and loved for exactly who we are.

119
00:15:57,106 --> 00:15:58,848
[SPEAKER_02]: This one's called In Her Closet.

120
00:16:06,424 --> 00:16:25,995
[SPEAKER_08]: In her closet she has dreams, shiny shoes and magazines Empty bottles of natural wine, and this is why she lies In her closet she has faith, mustard seed, unplanted state Skeletons and bullet rye, and this is why she cries

121
00:16:31,128 --> 00:16:57,560
[SPEAKER_08]: she cries oh she longs to live an honest life and as she grows it gets harder to hide the things in her closet holding back her life in her closet she has pain silk scarves and a silver chain sandals telling her to leave this is why she grieves

122
00:17:00,938 --> 00:17:05,560
[SPEAKER_08]: She has fear, cigarettes and madam's beer, hoodies from forgotten times.

123
00:17:05,600 --> 00:17:07,180
[SPEAKER_08]: This is why she cries.

124
00:17:07,400 --> 00:17:09,781
[SPEAKER_08]: And oh, she lies.

125
00:17:10,181 --> 00:17:13,642
[SPEAKER_08]: Oh, she cries.

126
00:17:14,743 --> 00:17:18,924
[SPEAKER_08]: Oh, she longs to live an honest life.

127
00:17:19,404 --> 00:17:26,707
[SPEAKER_08]: As she grows, it gets harder to hide the things in her closet holding

128
00:17:48,518 --> 00:17:57,405
[SPEAKER_08]: In her closet she has joy, formal gowns, her favorite toys, running shoes, grandma's meal coat, this is what she knows.

129
00:17:57,565 --> 00:18:07,712
[SPEAKER_08]: But in her closet she has stress, at a band and suits to press, dirty laundry on the floor, she knows she's worth more.

130
00:18:33,936 --> 00:18:35,358
[SPEAKER_08]: Thank you so much.

131
00:19:02,413 --> 00:19:04,934
[SPEAKER_02]: So this summer I've been playing at all different kinds of festivals.

132
00:19:05,314 --> 00:19:08,135
[SPEAKER_02]: Bluegrass festivals, Americana festivals, some jazz festivals.

133
00:19:08,615 --> 00:19:12,376
[SPEAKER_02]: And usually a lot of times the audience will say, do we have any jazz fans in the house?

134
00:19:12,856 --> 00:19:14,356
[SPEAKER_02]: But I know we do here, right?

135
00:19:16,877 --> 00:19:21,398
[SPEAKER_02]: So this next one, one of the things I love to do in my writing is write songs based off of standards that I love.

136
00:19:26,993 --> 00:19:32,258
[SPEAKER_02]: And so this one I wrote based off of a standard that I love called Everything Happens to Me.

137
00:19:32,278 --> 00:19:34,601
[SPEAKER_02]: Are we familiar with that?

138
00:19:34,641 --> 00:19:35,481
[SPEAKER_02]: Some folks familiar with it?

139
00:19:35,962 --> 00:19:41,467
[SPEAKER_02]: So it goes like, I make a date for golf and you can bet your life it rains.

140
00:19:42,408 --> 00:19:45,872
[SPEAKER_02]: I try to give a party and the guy upstairs complains.

141
00:19:46,592 --> 00:19:50,436
[SPEAKER_02]: I guess I'll go through life just catching colds and missing trains.

142
00:19:51,937 --> 00:19:53,519
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything happens to me.

143
00:19:54,379 --> 00:20:01,425
[SPEAKER_02]: So it's very woe is me, and then the bridge is like, oh, I lost my lady love, and she doesn't love me anymore, and woe is me, and everything's terrible.

144
00:20:02,066 --> 00:20:09,092
[SPEAKER_02]: So I went through something last year that made me feel like writing a woe is me song and was very inspired by that.

145
00:20:09,152 --> 00:20:11,233
[SPEAKER_02]: This is called Today is Not My Day.

146
00:20:11,253 --> 00:20:16,137
[SPEAKER_02]: We're just tuning up here.

147
00:20:37,295 --> 00:20:40,698
[SPEAKER_05]: Our instruments got used to the hot weather.

148
00:20:41,078 --> 00:20:41,159
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

149
00:20:41,199 --> 00:20:43,801
[SPEAKER_02]: All of our instruments are very angry right now.

150
00:20:44,181 --> 00:20:44,782
[SPEAKER_02]: OK.

151
00:20:44,802 --> 00:20:45,142
[SPEAKER_02]: We good?

152
00:20:45,162 --> 00:20:52,509
[SPEAKER_06]: Is that OK?

153
00:21:07,650 --> 00:21:08,771
[SPEAKER_02]: That's better.

154
00:21:08,951 --> 00:21:09,251
[SPEAKER_02]: All right.

155
00:21:09,772 --> 00:21:11,153
[SPEAKER_02]: It's called, Today Is Not My Day.

156
00:21:16,757 --> 00:21:20,560
[SPEAKER_08]: Woke up to gray skies that covered the blue.

157
00:21:21,601 --> 00:21:25,504
[SPEAKER_08]: Reached for the place where I used to find you.

158
00:21:26,384 --> 00:21:30,107
[SPEAKER_08]: Walked out the door with a thorn in my shoe.

159
00:21:30,948 --> 00:21:33,590
[SPEAKER_08]: Today is not my day.

160
00:21:35,868 --> 00:22:00,799
[SPEAKER_08]: ♪ Got to the car and my tire was flat ♪ ♪ Wonder what you would have done about that ♪ ♪ Hit every red light on the buses I sat ♪ ♪ Today is not my day ♪ ♪ Time surely heals all wounds ♪

161
00:22:03,682 --> 00:22:31,231
[SPEAKER_08]: But tomorrow can't come too soon Got home from work, left my keys in the door The candle burned out and it dripped on the floor I'm all out of wine, I can't cry anymore Today is not my day

162
00:22:53,167 --> 00:22:53,249
Bye!

163
00:23:23,260 --> 00:23:23,761
do do

164
00:23:46,380 --> 00:23:53,662
[SPEAKER_08]: Time surely heals all wounds, but tomorrow

165
00:24:11,826 --> 00:24:15,929
[SPEAKER_08]: I'll close my eyes and you're here next to me.

166
00:24:16,870 --> 00:24:20,873
[SPEAKER_08]: Everything's just as we hoped it would be.

167
00:24:21,694 --> 00:24:26,058
[SPEAKER_08]: Then the sun rises to shatter my dreams.

168
00:24:26,458 --> 00:24:31,442
[SPEAKER_08]: Today is not my day.

169
00:24:31,522 --> 00:24:35,345
[SPEAKER_08]: Today is not my day.

170
00:24:36,466 --> 00:24:40,049
[SPEAKER_08]: Today is not my day.

171
00:24:48,922 --> 00:24:49,842
[SPEAKER_02]: Jacob Means, mandolin.

172
00:24:51,863 --> 00:24:52,823
[SPEAKER_02]: Max Ridley on bass.

173
00:25:00,186 --> 00:25:01,966
[SPEAKER_02]: We got Grayson Hill on guitar.

174
00:25:05,247 --> 00:25:06,928
[SPEAKER_02]: Hector Falu Guzman on drums.

175
00:25:07,188 --> 00:25:09,589
[SPEAKER_06]: I said that right.

176
00:25:11,369 --> 00:25:12,910
[SPEAKER_02]: It's such a pleasure to play with these guys.

177
00:25:13,750 --> 00:25:16,331
[SPEAKER_02]: I've been playing with Jacob for, shoot,

178
00:25:19,291 --> 00:25:19,572
[SPEAKER_02]: 15 years?

179
00:25:19,592 --> 00:25:20,473
[SPEAKER_02]: Has it been that long?

180
00:25:21,334 --> 00:25:24,879
[SPEAKER_02]: We went to school together at New England Conservatory many moons ago.

181
00:25:24,899 --> 00:25:28,564
[SPEAKER_02]: And I've been playing with Max here for, I don't know, a long time too.

182
00:25:29,365 --> 00:25:32,028
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's just really special to share the stage with these guys.

